<bioghist encodinganalog="545$a">
<head>Biographical Information
</head>
<p>Charles Pitman Atkinson was born 29 January 1827 in Cumberland County, New Jersey, to Abbott Atkinson and Mary Atkinson.  By 1850, Charles Atkinson owned a farm in Salem County, New Jersey.  He enlisted in Company F, 3rd Regiment, Pennsylvania Heavy Artillery on 8 October 1862.  Atkinson was assigned to duty as a surgeon's steward, a position he retained through the rest of the Civil War.  After the war ended, Atkinson graduated from the Philadelphia University of Medicine and Surgery and became a doctor in Salem County.  He married Phebe Van Meter (1830-1918) in 1850, and they had three children.  Atkinson died 26 October 1907 and was buried in Olivet Cemetery in Salem County.
</p>
</bioghist>
<scopecontent encodinganalog="520$a">
<head>Scope and Content
</head>
<p>Letters, 1862-1865, from Charles Pitman Atkinson (1827-1907), a surgeon's steward at Fort Seward in Suffolk, Virginia, and at Fort Monroe, in Hampton, Virginia, discussing his duties, including service aboard a flag of truce ship and in Union army prison.  Atkinson comments on the war in camp and discusses the fall of Richmond, Virginia, and the surrender of the Army of Northern Virginia.  Atkinson provides advice to his wife Phebe Atkinson (1830-1918) on running the farm in Salem County, New Jersey and comments on his children.  Papers include Atkinson's discharge and a letter from his wife.
</p>
</scopecontent>
